Claude responded: When debugging Java, you should set breakpoints in the normal .
When debugging Java, you should set breakpoints in the normal .java source file, not the decompiled .class file. Here's why:

Breakpoints in .java source files work because:

The debugger maps source lines to bytecode via debug symbols (compiled in with javac -g)
IDEs like IntelliJ and Eclipse use the .java file's line numbers to pause execution at the right place
You get accurate variable names, readable stack traces, and reliable step-through behavior
Decompiled .class files are problematic because:

Decompiled code is a reconstruction — line numbers often don't match the original bytecode
Variable names may be mangled (e.g., var1, var2) since local variable info can be stripped
Some constructs (lambdas, inner classes, try-with-resources) decompile imperfectly, so breakpoints land in the wrong spot or not at all
The decompiler output is read-only in most IDEs, so you can't even set a proper breakpoint on it reliably
When you only have the .class file (no source): If you're debugging a library without source, your options are:

Attach the source JAR — most libraries publish a -sources.jar; attach it in your IDE for accurate breakpoints
Use the decompiled view cautiously — IntelliJ's "Decompile" feature does let you set breakpoints on decompiled code, but expect some misalignment
Bytecode-level debugging — tools like JDB or bytecode debuggers let you step through raw instructions if needed
The bottom line: always prefer the .java source file. If you don't have it, get the sources JAR before falling back to the decompiled .class.

