Claude responded: CI/CD Explained
CI/CD Explained
CI — Continuous Integration The practice of frequently merging code changes into a shared repo, where an automated pipeline immediately builds and tests the code. The goal is to catch bugs early.

A typical CI pipeline does things like:

Compile the code
Run unit/integration tests
Check code style/linting
Run static analysis (e.g. SonarQube)
CD — Continuous Delivery / Continuous Deployment Extends CI by also deploying the built artifact somewhere after tests pass.

Continuous Delivery — automatically prepares a release and deploys to a staging/pre-prod environment, but a human approves the final push to production
Continuous Deployment — fully automated all the way to production, no human gate
When someone says "it's only CI"
It means the pipeline stops after testing — it does not deploy anywhere. So:

✅ Code is built and tested automatically on every push/PR
❌ Nothing is deployed automatically — a developer has to manually deploy
This is common when:

The project is early-stage and doesn't have a deployment pipeline set up yet
Deployment is handled separately (e.g. manually, or by a different team/tool)
The repo is a library, not a deployable service, so "deploying" doesn't really apply
The CD part is intentionally gated behind manual steps for safety reasons
In short: CI = confidence that the code works, CD = actually shipping it somewhere.

