May 9
Claude responded: Yes!
Yes! Spring Boot has equivalents for both of those. Here's how they map:

items.xml equivalent → Schema Definition
In Spring Boot you have a few options:

Option 1 — Let Hibernate generate it (most common)
You just define your @Entity classes and Hibernate automatically creates the tables for you:

yaml
spring:
  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: create  # or update, create-drop, validate
ddl-auto value	What it does
create	Drops and recreates schema on every startup
update	Adds missing columns/tables, doesn't drop
create-drop	Creates on start, drops on shutdown
validate	Just checks schema matches entities, no changes
none	Does nothing
Option 2 — schema.sql (manual SQL, closest to items.xml)
You can write raw SQL to define your schema and Spring Boot runs it on startup:

src/main/resources/schema.sql
sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (
    id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
    username VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    email VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    password VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
);
Option 3 — Flyway / Liquibase (best for production)
These are database migration tools — the most robust option for real projects:

Flyway uses versioned SQL files:

src/main/resources/db/migration/
    V1__create_users_table.sql
    V2__add_bio_to_users.sql
    V3__create_articles_table.sql
Each file runs once and never again — Flyway tracks what's been applied. This is the closest equivalent to how items.xml manages schema evolution.

Impex equivalent → Data Seeding
Option 1 — data.sql (simplest, closest to impex)
src/main/resources/data.sql
sql
INSERT INTO roles (name) VALUES ('ROLE_USER');
INSERT INTO roles (name) VALUES ('ROLE_ADMIN');
Spring Boot runs this automatically on startup.

Option 2 — Flyway seed migrations
You can seed data in the same versioned migration files:

sql
-- V2__seed_roles.sql
INSERT INTO roles (name) VALUES ('ROLE_USER');
INSERT INTO roles (name) VALUES ('ROLE_ADMIN');
Option 3 — ApplicationRunner / CommandLineRunner (Java-based seeding)
For more complex seeding logic you can do it programmatically:

java
@Component
public class DataSeeder implements CommandLineRunner {

    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) {
        if (userRepository.count() == 0) {
            User admin = new User();
            admin.setUsername("admin");
            userRepository.save(admin);
        }
    }
}
How they map together
SAP Commerce Cloud	Spring Boot equivalent
items.xml (schema definition)	@Entity classes + ddl-auto, or schema.sql
items.xml (schema evolution)	Flyway / Liquibase migrations
Impex (data seeding)	data.sql, Flyway, or CommandLineRunner
For a production Spring Boot project the recommended combo is Flyway + ddl-auto: validate — Flyway manages your schema and Spring just validates that the DB matches your entities, which is the safest approach.

