MapStruct at Enterprise Level
Yes, MapStruct is very common at enterprise level. It's one of the most widely used mapping libraries in the Java/Spring ecosystem. You'll see it in large codebases because:

It generates mapping code at compile time (not reflection at runtime) so it's very fast
The generated code is plain Java — you can actually read and debug it
Works naturally with the DTO ↔ Entity pattern which every enterprise Spring app has
Integrates well with Spring's dependency injection
The Alternatives You'll See in Enterprise
Library	How it works	Common?
MapStruct	Compile-time code generation	Very common
ModelMapper	Runtime reflection	Common but falling out of favour
Manual mapping	You write it yourself	Common in smaller projects
BeanUtils (Spring)	Runtime reflection, simple cases	Occasionally
What is BeanMapper / BeanUtils
BeanUtils is a utility (from Spring or Apache Commons) that copies properties between two objects by matching field names:

java
// Spring's BeanUtils
UserEntity entity = new UserEntity();
BeanUtils.copyProperties(userDto, entity); 
// copies all fields where names match
It works by reflection at runtime — it looks at both objects, finds fields with the same name, and copies the values over.

The problem with BeanUtils
java
// These two classes
public class UserDto {
    private String username;
    private String email;
}

public class UserEntity {
    private String username;
    private String email;
    private String passwordHash; // different name
}
Fields with matching names get copied automatically ✅
Fields with different names get silently skipped ❌
Type mismatches cause runtime errors, not compile-time ❌
No IDE support — you won't know something broke until runtime ❌
MapStruct vs BeanUtils side by side
BeanUtils:

java
// simple but fragile
BeanUtils.copyProperties(source, destination);
MapStruct:

java
// you define the mapper
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface CommentMapper {

    @Mapping(source = "author.username", target = "authorName")
    CommentDto toDto(CommentEntity entity);

    CommentEntity toEntity(CommentDto dto);
}

// then just inject and use it
@Service
public class CommentsService {
    private final CommentMapper commentMapper;
    
    public ResponseEntity<Comment> getComment(Integer id) {
        CommentEntity entity = commentRepository.findById(id);
        Comment dto = commentMapper.toDto(entity);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(dto);
    }
}
Why MapStruct wins at enterprise level
Catches mapping errors at compile time, not runtime
Handles different field names explicitly with @Mapping
Generated code is readable and debuggable
No performance overhead from reflection
Works great with Lombok (another enterprise staple)
In short — BeanUtils is a quick and dirty solution, MapStruct is what you'd use in a production enterprise codebase.

