Claude responded: Before MapStruct
Before MapStruct
The most common approach was just manual mapping methods, usually inside the service or a dedicated mapper class:

java
// A dedicated mapper class, written by hand
public class CommentMapper {

    public static CommentDto toDto(CommentEntity entity) {
        CommentDto dto = new CommentDto();
        dto.setId(entity.getId());
        dto.setBody(entity.getBody());
        dto.setCreatedAt(entity.getCreatedAt());
        dto.setAuthorName(entity.getAuthor().getUsername());
        return dto;
    }

    public static CommentEntity toEntity(CommentDto dto) {
        CommentEntity entity = new CommentEntity();
        entity.setBody(dto.getBody());
        entity.setCreatedAt(dto.getCreatedAt());
        return entity;
    }
}
This was just pure Java — no library, no magic, just boring repetitive code.

The Problems With Manual Mapping
1. Extremely tedious
If you have 20 fields, you write 20 dto.setX(entity.getX()) lines. Then you write them again for the reverse direction. Then again for every other entity.

2. Silent bugs when you add a field
This is the biggest problem:

java
// You add a new field to your entity
@Entity
public class CommentEntity {
    private String body;
    private OffsetDateTime createdAt;
    private String newField; // ← added this
}

// But you forget to update the mapper
public static CommentDto toDto(CommentEntity entity) {
    dto.setBody(entity.getBody());
    dto.setCreatedAt(entity.getCreatedAt());
    // newField is silently never mapped ← no error, no warning
}
The compiler won't tell you. The app starts fine. You only find out when data is missing in your API response.

3. Scattered across the codebase
Sometimes mapping logic ended up inside services themselves:

java
@Service
public class CommentService {
    
    public CommentDto getComment(Long id) {
        CommentEntity entity = repository.findById(id);
        
        // mapping logic dumped right here in the service
        CommentDto dto = new CommentDto();
        dto.setId(entity.getId());
        dto.setBody(entity.getBody());
        // ...
        
        return dto;
    }
}
Services became bloated and hard to read.

What MapStruct Does Differently
You define the interface:

java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface CommentMapper {
    CommentDto toDto(CommentEntity entity);
    CommentEntity toEntity(CommentDto dto);
}
MapStruct generates this at compile time:

java
// Auto-generated by MapStruct - you never write this
@Component
public class CommentMapperImpl implements CommentMapper {

    @Override
    public CommentDto toDto(CommentEntity entity) {
        CommentDto dto = new CommentDto();
        dto.setId(entity.getId());
        dto.setBody(entity.getBody());
        dto.setCreatedAt(entity.getCreatedAt());
        return dto;
    }
}
The Key Advantages
Compile-time safety
If you add a field to your entity and don't handle it in the mapper, MapStruct warns you at compile time — the build fails, not your runtime.

Performance
Manual mapping and MapStruct are equally fast because MapStruct generates the same plain Java setter code. Compare this to ModelMapper or BeanUtils which use reflection at runtime and are significantly slower.

Handles complex mappings cleanly
java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface CommentMapper {

    // field names differ? just tell it
    @Mapping(source = "author.username", target = "authorName")
    @Mapping(source = "createdAt", target = "createdDate")
    CommentDto toDto(CommentEntity entity);

    // ignore fields going the other way
    @Mapping(target = "author", ignore = true)
    CommentEntity toEntity(CommentDto dto);
}
Composable mappers
java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring", uses = {UserMapper.class})
public interface CommentMapper {
    // automatically uses UserMapper to map the author field
    CommentDto toDto(CommentEntity entity);
}
Summary
Manual Mapping	MapStruct
Boilerplate	Tons	Minimal
Compile-time safety	❌	✅
Performance	Fast	equally Fast
New field added	Silent bug	Build error
Readable	Gets messy	Clean interface
Debug	Read your code	Read generated code
The bottom line — manual mapping works fine for tiny projects, but in an enterprise codebase with dozens of entities and DTOs, MapStruct saves significant time and prevents a whole class of bugs.

