Claude responded: Yes exactly!
Yes exactly! The Entity is the full DB representation and the DTO is only what you expose. Here's how MapStruct handles it:

The Typical Scenario
java
// Entity - full DB representation
@Entity
public class UserEntity {
    private Long id;
    private String username;
    private String email;
    private String passwordHash;  // never expose this
    private String bio;
    private String image;
    private OffsetDateTime createdAt;  // internal
    private OffsetDateTime updatedAt;  // internal
    private List<ArticleEntity> articles;  // DB relationship
}

// DTO - only what the API exposes
public class UserDto {
    private String username;
    private String email;
    private String bio;
    private String image;
}
How to Ignore Fields in MapStruct
Ignore on the mapper directly
java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface UserMapper {

    @Mapping(target = "passwordHash", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "createdAt", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "updatedAt", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "articles", ignore = true)
    UserEntity toEntity(UserDto dto);

    // entity → dto is easier, extra fields just don't exist on dto
    // MapStruct automatically ignores entity fields not present in dto
    UserDto toDto(UserEntity entity);
}
Notice that entity → dto you don't need to ignore anything. MapStruct is smart enough to just skip entity fields that don't have a matching field in the DTO.

It's only dto → entity where you need to explicitly ignore fields that exist on the entity but not the DTO.

Using @BeanMapping to ignore all unmapped by default
If you have many fields to ignore, instead of listing every single ignore = true you can flip the default:

java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface UserMapper {

    @BeanMapping(ignoreByDefault = true)
    @Mapping(target = "username", source = "username")
    @Mapping(target = "email", source = "email")
    UserEntity toEntity(UserDto dto);  // only maps what you explicitly list
}
Instead of saying "ignore these 10 fields", you say "only map these 2 fields" — much cleaner when the DTO is small relative to the entity.

Which approach to use
Situation	Approach
Entity and DTO are similar size	List ignore = true per field
DTO is much smaller than Entity	Use ignoreByDefault = true and list only what you want
Entity → DTO	Usually no ignores needed, MapStruct handles it
DTO → Entity	Almost always need ignores for DB-managed fields
Fields you'll almost always ignore when going DTO → Entity
java
@Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)           // DB generates this
@Mapping(target = "createdAt", ignore = true)    // set by DB or @PrePersist
@Mapping(target = "updatedAt", ignore = true)    // set by DB or @PrePersist
@Mapping(target = "passwordHash", ignore = true) // never comes from DTO, set separately
@Mapping(target = "articles", ignore = true)     // relationships handled separately
These are so common that ignoreByDefault = true often makes more sense for the DTO → Entity direction.

