## 1. Debugging — .class vs .java files
 
Always set breakpoints in the **`.java` source file**, not the decompiled `.class` file.
 
- The debugger maps source lines to bytecode via **debug symbols** compiled in with `javac -g`
- Decompiled `.class` files are reconstructions — line numbers often don't match, variable names may be mangled
- If you only have the `.class` file, attach the **sources JAR** instead (most libraries publish a `-sources.jar`)
**Options when you have no source:**
- Attach the `-sources.jar` in your IDE
- Use IntelliJ's decompile view cautiously (breakpoints may misalign)
- Use bytecode-level debugging with JDB as a last resort
---
 
## 2. Swagger / OpenAPI Generated Code
 
### The Controller
```java
@Controller
@RequestMapping("${openapi.realWorldConduit.base-path:/api}")
public class ArticlesApiController implements ArticlesApi {
 
    private final ArticlesApiDelegate delegate;
 
    public ArticlesApiController(@Autowired(required = false) ArticlesApiDelegate delegate) {
        this.delegate = Optional.ofNullable(delegate).orElse(new ArticlesApiDelegate() {});
    }
}
```
 
### The Anonymous Class Pattern
`new ArticlesApiDelegate() {}` is an **anonymous class**, not direct interface instantiation.
 
- `new ArticlesApiDelegate()` alone would fail — you can't instantiate an interface
- The `{}` creates a nameless class that implements the interface with no overrides
- It inherits all **default methods** from the interface (which return `501 Not Implemented`)
### Why it works — Null Object Pattern
- If a concrete delegate bean **is** wired by Spring → use it (your business logic)
- If **no** delegate is provided → fall back to anonymous class → avoids `NullPointerException`
### What OpenAPI Generates
 
```
com.conduit.openapi.model/
    Comment.java        ← DTO / API shape
    Profile.java
    Article.java
 
com.conduit.openapi.api/
    ArticlesApi.java            ← interface with endpoint definitions
    ArticlesApiController.java  ← generated controller (don't touch)
    ArticlesApiDelegate.java    ← you implement this
```
 
