## 10. OpenAPI Models vs Entities
 
OpenAPI generates **two separate concerns**:
 
### DTO / Model classes (API shape)
```java
public class Comment {
    private Integer id;
    private OffsetDateTime createdAt;
    private String body;
    private Profile author;  // nested POJO
}
```
 
### What you still need to create (DB shape)
```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "comments")
public class CommentEntity {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String body;
    private OffsetDateTime createdAt;
 
    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "author_id")
    private UserEntity author;  // DB relationship
}
```
 
### Key differences
 
| OpenAPI DTO | Entity |
|---|---|
| No JPA annotations | `@Entity`, `@Table`, `@Id` etc |
| Nested objects are POJOs | Nested objects are `@ManyToOne` / `@OneToMany` |
| `Integer` id | Usually `Long` id |
| No DB concerns | Has `@Column`, indexes etc |
 
### Entity generation options
- **IntelliJ Ultimate** — reverse engineer entities from a connected DB
- **JPA Buddy** (IntelliJ plugin) — most recommended, generates entities from DTOs or DB, generates MapStruct mappers and repositories
- **Lombok** — not a generator but drastically reduces boilerplate
### Lombok example
```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "comments")
@Data
@NoArgsConstructor
@AllArgsConstructor
@Builder
public class CommentEntity {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String body;
 
    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "author_id")
    private UserEntity author;
}
```
 
