## 12. Ignoring Fields in MapStruct
 
Entities typically have **more fields** than DTOs. Here's how to handle the difference.
 
### Entity → DTO (easier direction)
MapStruct automatically skips entity fields not present in the DTO. No ignores needed.
 
```java
UserDto toDto(UserEntity entity);  // just works
```
 
### DTO → Entity (needs explicit ignores)
Fields that exist on the entity but not the DTO must be ignored:
 
```java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface UserMapper {
 
    @Mapping(target = "passwordHash", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "createdAt", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "updatedAt", ignore = true)
    @Mapping(target = "articles", ignore = true)
    UserEntity toEntity(UserDto dto);
}
```
 
### ignoreByDefault (when DTO is much smaller than Entity)
Instead of listing every ignored field, flip the default — only map what you explicitly list:
 
```java
@BeanMapping(ignoreByDefault = true)
@Mapping(target = "username", source = "username")
@Mapping(target = "email", source = "email")
UserEntity toEntity(UserDto dto);
```
 
### Fields you'll almost always ignore (DTO → Entity)
 
```java
@Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)           // DB generates this
@Mapping(target = "createdAt", ignore = true)    // set by @PrePersist
@Mapping(target = "updatedAt", ignore = true)    // set by @PrePersist
@Mapping(target = "passwordHash", ignore = true) // set separately, never from DTO
@Mapping(target = "articles", ignore = true)     // relationships handled separately
```
 
### Which approach to use
 
| Situation | Approach |
|---|---|
| Entity and DTO are similar size | List `ignore = true` per field |
| DTO is much smaller than Entity | Use `ignoreByDefault = true` |
| Entity → DTO direction | Usually no ignores needed |
| DTO → Entity direction | Almost always need ignores |
 
